WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:03.210
SPEAKER: Key Visualizer
is a visualization tool

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.210
that helps you analyze the
access patterns in your Cloud

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.230
Bigtable instance.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.310
The Cloud Bigtable console lets
you monitor your application

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:15.020
with line charts, which is
effective in most cases.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.410
However, how would you know
what is causing high CPU,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.050
if there are any
hotspots or large rows,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.500
or if a MapReduce is effecting
your serving latency?

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.090
That's why we are announcing Key
Visualizer for Cloud Bigtable.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.390
It shows you read and write
patterns in two dimensions--

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.900
over time and across keyspace.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.610
It helps you find
or prevent hotspots.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.030
It helps you find rows
with too much data, which

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.280
can cause performance issues.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.500
And you can see if your
key schema is balanced.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.110
Key Visualizer has a heat map
that shows time horizontally.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.970
In this example,
we see the number

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.940
of operations over one week.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.740
The keyspace is
shown vertically.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.030
A heat scale shows values
of a selected metric.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.830
For example, ops-- from
cold, representing zero,

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.240
over blue, to purple,
to red, to yellow,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.700
to glowing white for
very high values.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.240
The tool tip will
show us details

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.000
about this metric for a
particular time and key range.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.960
In this example, notice
that some key ranges,

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.920
like the one at the bottom,
exhibit a diurnal pattern,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.000
whereas, other
ranges are very cold.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.220
Also notice the
white glowing line

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.690
in the middle that shows
the busiest key range.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.490
The diagonal lines
represent linear scans

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.450
possibly from a MapReduce job.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.690
The bar chart at the
bottom shows average values

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.200
across all visible keys
in 15-minute intervals.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.190
Similarly, the bar
chart on the right

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.240
shows the averages over
time per key range.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.580
This peak, again,
shows our busiest key.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.900
Bigtable developers often create
keys with a prefix hierarchy.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.790
In this example,
we see keys that

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:06.120
are constructed with delimiters,
such as slashes or dashes.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.870
Key Visualizer has a
heuristic that automatically

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:13.670
extracts the prefix tree from
your key schema, if available.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:17.480
The key prefix hierarchy
is shown on the left side.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.510
We can see that the
behavior of a table

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:25.100
often correlates with the key
prefixes, as in this example.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.100
When we click on
a prefix, it will

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.410
zoom into the
corresponding key range.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.706
We can also zoom and pan
by using the scroll wheel

00:02:33.706 --> 00:02:34.205
or dragging.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.100
Key Visualizer offers
you these 11 metrics.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.970
Ops is the sum of read
and write operations.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.380
The three warnings metrics--

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.860
read or write pressure
index and large rows--

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.520
can discover problem areas.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.930
Then, we have read metrics,
write metrics, latency,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.850
and the amount of data stored.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.240
Key Visualizer automatically
analyzes the performance

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.590
of your Bigtable instance.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.790
This may involve trillions
of metric values.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.980
Not only does Key
Visualizer aggregate

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.410
all of these metric values to
visualize them in the browser,

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.860
it also performs a
detailed analysis

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.850
on your table's performance.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.260
This produces the
following metrics--

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.380
read pressure, which is an
opaque function of read CPU,

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.080
read key length, which is the
number of read ops waiting

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.680
to be executed, and latency.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.420
Write pressure is
a similar function

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.740
to detect bad performance for
writing and large rows, which

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:43.690
will flag any row that contains
more than 1 gigabyte of data.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.010
The result, shown in this view,
lets us discover and prevent

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:49.690
hotspots.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:53.050
Best of all, these computations
won't cause any performance

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.000
degradation on your table.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.280
And all this is free.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.550
Let's now see how we
made this easy for you.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.710
Key Visualizer opens by
default with the ops metric.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.640
This gives us an overview of
the activity in your table.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.540
Then, you check if there are
any warnings at the top bar.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.760
If there aren't,
that's good news.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.959
Your table's performance
is, most likely, fine.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:21.870
This example has three warnings.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.060
One of them is
severe, as indicated

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.110
by the three exclamation marks.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.750
Clicking on the Read
Pressure Index warning

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.240
brings us to the
Read Pressure metric,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.490
where we see immediately where
and when the problem happened.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.900
We now zoom into the
white glowing stripe.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.660
The tool tip shows us which
keys are causing problems.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.420
At the top, we see
the worst problem.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.370
It's a single key
that has been stressed

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.740
for more than three hours.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.310
We can see the precise
name of the offending key.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.070
We can see that it was using
nearly 600 million nodes

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.600
worth of CPU, which is more
than half of a server's CPU.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.350
And it tells us what caused
this bad performance.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:11.190
This row contains 9.22
gigabytes of data,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.040
which is way more than
the recommended 256

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.090
megabytes per row.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.360
In all of these
examples, we were looking

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.730
at one metric at a time.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:28.680
Very often, however, we want
to compare different metrics.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.080
Rather than switching
between metrics,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.940
we can show all the
metrics and their values

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:36.550
together, on the right.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.280
When we expand these, we
can see all these metrics

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.380
in miniature views.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.810
The zoom and pan operations
are synchronized,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.860
so we could compare
details for each symmetric.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.600
Key Visualizer
for Cloud Bigtable

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.850
visualizes the key
access patterns

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.980
in your table, helps you find
hotspots, and large rows.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.880
It extracts the key prefix
hierarchy, if available.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.240
Key Visualizer analyzes
trillions of metric values

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.030
to point you to problem areas.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.330
And it does this without any
impact on your Cloud Bigtable

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:11.380
performance.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

